# DGen Registry Introduction

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:erlfdb, "~> 1.0"},
  {:dgen, path: "/Users/jstimpson/dev/dgen"}
])
```

## Intro

`DGen.Registry` is a distributed Elixir process registry that is built to gracefully and automatically handle nodes joining and leaving your cluster.

If you're already running `DGen`, all you have to do is

1. Ensure your nodes are capable of communication (e.g. VM config, network, cookie)
2. Add a new child to your supervision tree

As long as you do this, `DGen.Registry` will take care of clustering the Elixir VMs with each other and will ensure that the dataset they share (namely process registrations) is consistent across the cluster.

Nodes can join and leave at your will. `DGen.Registry` keeps everything up-to-date.

> **Reminder:** The `DGen` project is a set of OTP utilities that inherit their distributed consensus from a backend database. As of the time of writing, the single supported backend is FoundationDB. Thus, `DGen.Registry` requires that your application connects to a running FoundationDB cluster.

## What this document covers

This document, an interactive Livebook, covers the "Getting Started" of `DGen.Registry`, including how to register processes and metadata and how to query them.

For a discussion of the consistency guarantees of `DGen.Registry`, please see the [:dgen_registry Design Doc](dgen_registry_design.html)

## Getting Started

All of `DGen`'s utilities are assumed to live in a multi-tenant application, so the data tenancy is the first item to set up. For this Livebook, we'll start a local FoundationDB sandbox, and we'll create a tenant called `"my_app"` that will store all of our data moving forward.

```elixir
tenant = :dgen_erlfdb.sandbox_open("livebook", "my_app")
```

Next, we start our process registry inside this tenant. We can choose any atom name for it. Here, we go with `:my_app_registry`. The name is registered on the node itself, the entrypoint for other processes to register themselves. All nodes that wish to access this registry will start this same process, and `DGen.Registry` will cluster them all together, treating the group as a single unit.

```elixir
DGen.Registry.start_link(:my_app_registry, tenant)
```

We'll define a simple GenServer that will be registered. For demo purposes, We're going to model a set of `Player`s and allow them to be in `Room`s, like an online game lobby, for instance.

```elixir
defmodule Player do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(name) do
    GenServer.start_link(
      __MODULE__,
      %{},
      name: {:via, DGen.Registry, {:my_app_registry, name}}
    )
  end

  def move_to_room(name, room) do
    DGen.Registry.set_metadata(
      {:my_app_registry, name},
      %{index: %{room: room}, data: %{}}
    )
  end

  @impl true
  def init(_) do
    {:ok, %{}}
  end
end

defmodule Room do
  def list_players(room) do
    DGen.Registry.query(:my_app_registry, %{room: room})
    |> Enum.map(fn %{name: name, pid: pid} -> {name, pid} end)
  end
end
```

Start the first player: `"alice"`.

```elixir
Player.start_link("alice")
```

We can confirm that `"alice"`'s pid is retrievable from normal OTP utilities.

```elixir
GenServer.whereis({:via, DGen.Registry, {:my_app_registry, "alice"}})
```

Calling `move_to_room` will set some indexed metadata on the process registration. We're using this to represent the fact that Alice is in room 37.

```elixir
Player.move_to_room("alice", 37)
```

```elixir
Room.list_players(37)
```

Start player 2: `"bob"`, and move him into room 37 as well.

```elixir
Player.start_link("bob")
```

```elixir
Player.move_to_room("bob", 37)
```

```elixir
Room.list_players(37)
```

Finally, we'll stop Bob's process and observe that the list of players in the room updates automatically via OTP `:DOWN` events.

```elixir
GenServer.stop({:via, DGen.Registry, {:my_app_registry, "bob"}})
```

```elixir
Room.list_players(37)
```
